sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch01-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Apart from their lesser uniformity, their often monstrous development of one part, and their fertility when crossed, domestic races of a species differ from each other in the same manner as closely allied species of a genus in nature, though in most cases to a lesser degree.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Domestic races differ like congeneric species, but usually less";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter I, “Variation under Domestication”; section “Character of Domestic Varieties”. Scope: domestic races compared with closely allied wild congeneric species"
}
